    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Cardiac development, physiology and function depend on the dynamic control of tissue specific gene expression by distant acting transcriptional enhancers. Super enhancers (SEs) are recently discovered enhancer clusters in the mammalian genome with unusually strong enrichment of various transcriptional co-activators (Young et al., 2013). Although recent studies suggested that the SEs are critical for development and disease mechanism, the role of cardiac-specific SEs is still unclear.\r\nHere, I identified a potential SE that regulates the transcription of cardiac transcription factor, NKX2.5, and the tropomyosin-binding subunit of the troponin complex, TNNT2, during cardiomyocyte (CM) differentiation. I utilized the chemically defined monolayer method to generate beating CMs from human embryonic stem cells (hESCs). Chromatin immune-precipitation sequencing (ChIP-seq) and RNA sequencing (RNA-seq) of each stage of CM differentiation revealed the target super enhancer to be located upstream of the NKX2.5 gene. I implemented the CRISPR/Cas9 system to generate mutant hESC lines by deletion of target super enhancer (NKX2.5SE-/-). The expression of NKX2.5 and TNNT2 genes was severely decreased in NKX2.5SE-/- lines during CM differentiation, demonstrating the effect of the candidate SE in transcriptional regulation of key cardiac marker genes.\r\nCollectively, our current results and further studies may help identify the potential mechanisms of crucial cardiac-specific SEs that regulate cardiac development through transcriptional regulation of key regulatory genes.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
